WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.511
This chapter covers every lung sound on the study guide and what causes each one.

2
00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:14.600
We start with the three normal breath sounds and the rule that a normal sound in the wrong place is abnormal.

3
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:26.252
Then we cover diminished and absent sounds, fine and coarse crackles, wheezes, rhonchi and stridor, the pleural friction rub, grunting, and the voice sounds.

4
00:00:26.252 --> 00:00:38.200
We finish by putting the sounds together with the other chest findings, condition by condition, so you can tell a pneumothorax from an effusion from atelectasis.

5
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:49.039
A breath sound is normal only at its proper location, and the deck says to learn the location more than the description.

6
00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:53.443
Vesicular sounds belong over the peripheral lung fields.

7
00:00:53.443 --> 00:00:59.421
They are soft and low pitched, and inspiration lasts longer than expiration.

8
00:00:59.421 --> 00:01:07.287
Bronchovesicular sounds belong over the main bronchi, at the upper sternum and between the scapulae.

9
00:01:07.287 --> 00:01:14.602
They are moderate in pitch and intensity, with inspiration and expiration about equally long.

10
00:01:14.602 --> 00:01:21.995
Bronchial sounds, which Hess calls bronchial or tracheal, or tubular, belong over the trachea.

11
00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:29.625
They are loud and high pitched, and expiration is as long as or slightly longer than inspiration.

12
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:37.305
The same sound can be normal or abnormal depending on where you hear it.

13
00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:46.191
Bronchial breath sounds out in the lung periphery mean denser lung is carrying tracheal sound outward.

14
00:01:46.191 --> 00:01:49.849
They suggest consolidation or atelectasis.

15
00:01:49.849 --> 00:02:07.097
Consolidated lung fills with fluid, mucus, pus or cellular debris, and that dense tissue transmits the large airway sound to the chest wall, where normally you would hear only soft vesicular sounds.

16
00:02:07.097 --> 00:02:21.558
One conflict to know: a Hess figure description calls vesicular sounds high pitched and breezy, but Hess Table one dash two and the RCP 103 deck both say low pitched.

17
00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:24.258
Use low pitched, from the deck.

18
00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:34.141
Breath sounds get quieter when less air moves, or when sound is blocked from reaching the chest wall.

19
00:02:34.141 --> 00:02:47.649
The deck's causes are shallow or slow breathing, obstructed airways or hyperinflated lungs as in asthma and COPD, air or fluid in the pleural space, and obesity.

20
00:02:47.649 --> 00:02:49.915
Hess sorts them by pattern.

21
00:02:49.915 --> 00:03:00.906
Diffusely diminished sounds come from shallow breathing, or from hyperinflation with low airflow, as in emphysema and acute asthma.

22
00:03:00.906 --> 00:03:14.582
Localized decreases come from airway obstruction, atelectasis or main stem intubation, and decreased sounds at the bases are common with postoperative atelectasis.

23
00:03:14.582 --> 00:03:22.805
Unilateral decreases point to bronchial intubation, pneumothorax, atelectasis or pleural effusion.

24
00:03:22.805 --> 00:03:29.181
And in asthma, a silent chest means airflow is so low the wheeze disappears.

25
00:03:29.181 --> 00:03:31.195
It is never improvement.

26
00:03:31.195 --> 00:03:36.817
It is a late, critical warning that respiratory arrest is imminent.

27
00:03:38.233 --> 00:03:50.720
Adventitious sounds are extra sounds laid over the normal breath sounds: rhonchi, wheezes, crackles, which are also called rales, and stridor.

28
00:03:50.720 --> 00:04:01.449
RCP 103 calls rhonchi an outdated term, but RCP 203 chapter two still lists it, and crackles is the preferred modern term.

29
00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:05.230
Crackles are discontinuous, popping sounds.

30
00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:14.375
Fine crackles are high pitched, come at the end of inspiration, and are heard most often over the bases.

31
00:04:14.375 --> 00:04:21.586
They come from terminal airways or fluid filled or collapsed alveoli popping open.

32
00:04:21.586 --> 00:04:26.334
Coarse crackles come from excessive airway secretions.

33
00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:36.183
Hess describes them as air moving through fluid in the large airways, usually cleared by suctioning or coughing.

34
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:50.047
Fine crackles are the deck's hallmark sign of pulmonary edema, and Hess adds that crackles climbing higher up the lung fields mean worsening heart failure.

35
00:04:50.047 --> 00:04:59.523
They also appear in ARDS, in atelectasis, and in interstitial lung disease, where small airways reopen in stiff lungs.

36
00:04:59.523 --> 00:05:05.947
Hess describes bibasilar, Velcro-like crackles in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.

37
00:05:05.947 --> 00:05:16.708
In pneumonia you hear crackles over the involved lobe, and infants with respiratory distress syndrome or pneumonia have fine crackles.

38
00:05:16.708 --> 00:05:23.454
Coarse crackles appear in the decks' bronchiectasis, COPD and pulmonary edema cases.

39
00:05:23.454 --> 00:05:31.725
And coarse crackles heard over the trachea mean retained secretions, which is an indication to suction.

40
00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:43.022
A wheeze is continuous, musical and whistling, mainly expiratory, though Hess says it can be heard throughout the cycle.

41
00:05:43.022 --> 00:05:50.769
It comes from narrowed or obstructed airways: smooth muscle constriction, wall edema or mucus.

42
00:05:50.769 --> 00:06:03.213
Think asthma, COPD and bronchiectasis, and Hess adds heart failure and foreign body aspiration, where a child may have a wheeze over one isolated area.

43
00:06:03.213 --> 00:06:08.404
Rhonchi are deep, low pitched and rumbling, more on expiration.

44
00:06:08.404 --> 00:06:14.667
They come from secretions in the larger airways and may clear with coughing.

45
00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:17.304
Hess adds airway spasm or tumor.

46
00:06:17.304 --> 00:06:29.583
Stridor is a crowing, high pitched, inspiratory sound from upper airway obstruction, as in croup, post-extubation laryngeal edema and tracheomalacia.

47
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.529
The location of the sound points to the treatment.

48
00:06:35.529 --> 00:06:41.508
Wheezing comes from the bronchi, so it calls for a bronchodilator.

49
00:06:41.508 --> 00:06:53.102
Stridor comes from swelling in the upper airway, so it calls for racemic epinephrine, which RCP 105 mentions after bronchoscopy.

50
00:06:53.102 --> 00:07:01.707
In a severe asthma attack, the wheeze becomes both inspiratory and expiratory, called biphasic.

51
00:07:01.707 --> 00:07:05.421
RCP 203 Lesson 6b gives a clean contrast.

52
00:07:05.421 --> 00:07:12.124
Wheezes are high pitched and musical, usually from narrowed small airways.

53
00:07:12.124 --> 00:07:23.900
Rhonchi are low pitched and snoring-like, usually from larger airways with secretions, and they may clear or change after a cough.

54
00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:27.186
Pause and work this out.

55
00:07:27.186 --> 00:07:35.593
Shortly after extubation, your patient develops a high pitched, crowing sound that you hear on inspiration.

56
00:07:35.593 --> 00:07:41.800
What is the sound, where is it coming from, and what does the deck give for it?

57
00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:48.700
[6-second practice pause]

58
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.486
That is stridor.

59
00:07:50.486 --> 00:08:01.656
A crowing, high pitched, inspiratory sound means upper airway obstruction, and post-extubation laryngeal edema is one of its listed causes.

60
00:08:01.656 --> 00:08:09.773
Because the problem is swelling in the upper airway, the deck pairs stridor with racemic epinephrine.

61
00:08:09.773 --> 00:08:20.300
Compare it with a wheeze, which is musical, mainly expiratory, comes from narrowed bronchi, and calls for a bronchodilator instead.

62
00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:29.033
A pleural friction rub comes from inflamed pleural surfaces that no longer glide smoothly.

63
00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:40.195
Hess describes a grating sound, like two pieces of leather rubbing together, localized to the irritated area, and it may be intermittent.

64
00:08:40.195 --> 00:08:55.675
It is heard occasionally with pleural effusion, with pulmonary embolism when the infarction reaches the pleura, with interstitial lung disease, pleural infection, and after thoracic surgery.

65
00:08:55.675 --> 00:09:03.904
Grunting is heard in newborns in respiratory distress, who close the glottis to maintain lung volume.

66
00:09:03.904 --> 00:09:12.051
Voice sounds get louder and clearer over consolidation, because dense tissue transmits sound better.

67
00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:15.799
In bronchophony, ninety nine is clearly heard.

68
00:09:15.799 --> 00:09:18.651
In egophony, an e sounds like an a.

69
00:09:18.651 --> 00:09:24.517
In whispered pectoriloquy, a whispered one, two, three is clearly heard.

70
00:09:24.517 --> 00:09:29.650
In atelectasis, whispered pectoriloquy is diminished or absent.

71
00:09:31.067 --> 00:09:36.420
The study guide fills one gap from Heuer's table two dash sixteen.

72
00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:43.719
Early inspiratory crackles are scanty, transmitted to the mouth and not affected by cough.

73
00:09:43.719 --> 00:09:51.343
They come from the sudden opening of atelectatic bronchi, in bronchitis, emphysema and asthma.

74
00:09:51.343 --> 00:09:58.562
Late inspiratory crackles are diffuse and fine, appearing first in the dependent regions.

75
00:09:58.562 --> 00:10:08.538
They come from the sudden opening of collapsed peripheral airways, in atelectasis, pneumonia, pulmonary edema and fibrosis.

76
00:10:08.538 --> 00:10:16.973
Heuer also describes a pleural friction rub as creaking or grating, mainly on inhalation, from pleurisy.

77
00:10:16.973 --> 00:10:23.056
This is Heuer's version, and the instructor's slide forty seven may differ.

78
00:10:23.056 --> 00:10:34.492
The slide's own tables are images, and no source describes monophonic versus polyphonic wheezes, so check the textbook pages the slide cites.

79
00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:40.003
Now put the sounds together with the other findings.

80
00:10:40.003 --> 00:10:47.657
Asthma and COPD give wheezing, diminished sounds and a prolonged expiration, with hyperresonance.

81
00:10:47.657 --> 00:10:58.626
Consolidation and pneumonia give bronchial sounds, crackles and whispered pectoriloquy, with increased fremitus and a dull percussion note.

82
00:10:58.626 --> 00:11:05.806
A pneumothorax gives absent breath sounds, with the trachea pushed away and hyperresonance.

83
00:11:05.806 --> 00:11:17.958
A pleural effusion gives absent or diminished sounds, and occasionally a rub, with the trachea pushed away, a dull note, and decreased or absent fremitus.

84
00:11:17.958 --> 00:11:23.719
Notice that the pneumothorax and the effusion both push the trachea away.

85
00:11:23.719 --> 00:11:29.400
The percussion note separates them: hyperresonant air versus dull fluid.

86
00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:43.703
Atelectasis gives absent or diminished sounds, bronchial in some cases, and crackles, with the trachea pulled toward the affected side and a dull note.

87
00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:47.036
Upper airway obstruction gives stridor.

88
00:11:47.036 --> 00:12:01.392
Pulmonary edema gives crackles, which the deck notes are called rales, and wheezes, and the RCP 203 notes list grunting, gurgling or wheezing sounds among its symptoms.

89
00:12:01.392 --> 00:12:04.810
Hess Table one dash three adds two more.

90
00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:16.089
Emphysema gives decreased or absent sounds with no adventitious sounds, and pulmonary fibrosis gives vesicular sounds with crackles.

91
00:12:16.089 --> 00:12:27.625
The key contrast to remember: atelectasis pulls the trachea toward the lesion, while a pneumothorax or a large effusion pushes it away.

92
00:12:29.033 --> 00:12:29.760
One more.

93
00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:40.822
On one side of the chest, breath sounds are absent, the percussion note is hyperresonant, and the trachea is shifted away from that side.

94
00:12:40.822 --> 00:12:46.958
Which condition is this, and which finding rules out its closest look-alike?

95
00:12:47.867 --> 00:12:53.867
[6-second practice pause]

96
00:12:54.367 --> 00:12:56.267
This is a pneumothorax.

97
00:12:56.267 --> 00:13:07.341
Absent sounds and a trachea pushed away also fit a large pleural effusion, but an effusion is dull to percussion, because it is fluid.

98
00:13:07.341 --> 00:13:12.300
Hyperresonance means air, so the percussion note decides it.

99
00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:19.242
Atelectasis is ruled out too, because it pulls the trachea toward the affected side.

100
00:13:20.667 --> 00:13:23.539
Here is what to take into the exam.

101
00:13:23.539 --> 00:13:33.139
Learn the location of each normal sound, because bronchial sounds in the periphery mean consolidation or atelectasis.

102
00:13:33.139 --> 00:13:37.652
A silent chest in asthma is a warning, not improvement.

103
00:13:37.652 --> 00:13:46.678
Fine crackles mean alveoli popping open, the hallmark of pulmonary edema, and coarse crackles mean secretions.

104
00:13:46.678 --> 00:13:57.427
Wheezes come from the bronchi and call for a bronchodilator; stridor comes from the upper airway and calls for racemic epinephrine.

105
00:13:57.427 --> 00:14:00.956
Voice sounds get louder over consolidation.

106
00:14:00.956 --> 00:14:14.167
And by condition, use the trachea and the percussion note: a pneumothorax is hyperresonant, an effusion is dull, and atelectasis pulls the trachea toward itself.
